{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791579952,"nonce":"7++ZaVL2jSBNtuuf6BbOCgQ7S2A=","title":"Decode function bodies by chunk so a large, mostly skipped body stays small","message":"After lazy decoding (aadde3e), ashui's largest decoded body is Library.use(): 58,865 ops of a macro-built stylesheet behind `if (used) return`, called on every component creation. After the first call each call executes four ops, yet the whole body (about 2.4 MiB of Opcode plus 0.5 MiB of debug pairs) stays decoded.\n\nThe decoder already walks every body at load; it could record the byte offset of every 256th op, so the interpreter's step loop decodes only the chunks it reaches (`op_at(pc)`), while everything else keeps reading `ops()` whole. Debug pairs restart per function, so a trace would decode the debug table alone.","files":null}]}